{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:critical-dyadic-band","Reduction to the nearest dyadic\nboundary","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></math></span> be\nnonnegative integers with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></math></span> be the largest <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> for which <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>. The set is nonempty, so\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></math></span> is well defined, including when\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>E</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>or</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mspace width=\"35mm\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></math></span>\nthe first alternative holds by maximality. For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></math></span>, the left side of the required\ninequality increases with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> and its\nright side decreases, so the condition at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></math></span> implies all the others. This is an\nelementary reduction from <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>\ninequalities to one. Specialized to the seam reset charge (where the\nactual upper-reset condition gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>, as explained in\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c12\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c12\">6.80</a>), the reduced\nhypothesis at the single index <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></math></span>\nis proved logically equivalent to Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:upper-reset-band\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:upper-reset-band\">6.10</a>’s\nband-avoidance hypothesis.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:final-middle-cell","Excluding minus three at a final middle\ntransition","<p><em> Suppose that row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span> is a middle transition in\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:dynamics\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:dynamics\">5.16</a> and\nevery transition at a row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>\nis right. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>≠</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThis excludes one particular value under the stated tail assumption; it\nneither excludes every final middle transition nor proves the\nall-middle-row hypothesis of Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:two-sided-dyadic\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:two-sided-dyadic\">6.9</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:skipped-endpoint-trichotomy","The signed position of a skipped greedy\nprefix","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span> be omitted\nby the real greedy support <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span>, and\nput </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>s</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Exactly one\nof the following holds: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\"4pt\" columnalign=\"left left left\" data-frame-styles=\"\" framespacing=\".5em .125em\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus the real greedy prefix is one of the two\nadjacent integer words. This classifies its signed distance <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> from <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span>; it does not exclude the negative\ncase. Nor does it identify <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></math></span> with\nthe different coordinate <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> used in the\nfinal-middle argument.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:reverse-carry-word","Spacing of reverse-carry words","<p><em> For\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, let integer sequences <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span> satisfy </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Let\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> be integers. Suppose that\nat index <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> the coefficients agree\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Suppose also\nthat both coefficients and bits agree at indices <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The factor in\nparentheses is an odd integer. If the two terminal carries have absolute\nvalues at most <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></span>,\nrespectively, then <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></span>.\nFor a common bound <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi></math></span> the conclusion\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></math></span>, not <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span>. The formula follows by\nsubtracting the two recurrences: the initial bit difference gives the\nodd factor, and each subsequent agreement doubles it. An application\nmust supply the stated agreements and terminal bounds.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:linear-channel-nogo","Linear functionals factoring through one\nvalue","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>V</mi></math></span> be a vector\nspace over <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ev</mi></mrow><mo>:</mo><mi>V</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span> be linear, and\nchoose <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ev</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Suppose that each\nlinear functional <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>V</mi><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span> vanishes on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>ev</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ev</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>ev</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\ngives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>ev</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nConsequently any finite evaluation matrix <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> is an outer product\nand has rank at most one. Every square minor of order at least two\ntherefore vanishes. This excludes determinant arguments formed from\nthese particular functionals, not determinant methods with additional\nindependent information.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:two-thirds-band","Two-thirds band: exact localisation of\npost-take skip-unsafety","<p><em> Write a positive residual as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>R</mi></math></span>. A skipped rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> passes the sufficient dyadic test\nprecisely when <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></math></span>. Suppose a\nweight at rank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi></math></span> is taken without\nexhausting the residual: with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, assume <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span>. The new reciprocal residual\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. If the next take is at\nrank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>. The last skipped rank is\ndyadically unsafe exactly when </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>equivalently</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Clearing the\npositive denominators proves the equivalence. The interval has width\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>For a single skipped rank, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. The width is then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></math></span>, and the\ninterval lies inside <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>. In this single-skip\ncase, the cited corollaries exclude integral <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>R</mi></math></span> and show that a reduced pre-take\nresidual <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span> odd can be unsafe only if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></math></span>. The latter conclusion uses the\ndivisibility by <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>6</mn><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></math></span> under the unsafe-band\ninequalities.</em></p>\n<p><em>These are local transition statements, not bounds on the actual\nhalf-greedy orbit. Dyadic safety is only sufficient for the current tail\ntest. The odd-coprime data <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>41</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> give an unsafe\nsingle-skip example; no conclusion that the half-greedy orbit avoids\nsuch data follows from the band calculation.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:sharp-fatal-gap","A weaker sufficient test against the\nremaining tail","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span> be\npositive integers, and put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>u</mi></math></span>. Suppose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, equivalently that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span> and the greedy rule\nskips weight <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span>. The dyadic\nsufficient test <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> is\nequivalent to <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></math></span>. The weaker\nsufficient condition </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nensures <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span>, by comparison\nwith <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span>.\nThis conclusion excludes a fatal tail-mass deficit at the current step;\nit does not assert that <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi></math></span> is\nrepresentable by the remaining weights, or that all future greedy steps\nsurvive.</em></p>\n<p><em>The containment of these sufficient conditions is strict even for\nvalid rational data: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></span>. In contrast, the\nscalar pair <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> in the\nlinked inequality lemma does not arise from integral <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></span> under the present parity relation. Unit\nnumerators are nonfatal at such a skipped step, since then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Conversely, a fatal step requires\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></math></span>, hence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, or <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> is odd.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:gap-mass-summability","Summability of one gap length per level","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. The sum of one gap\nlength per level satisfies, for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mn>8</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThis follows by summing the per-level upper bounds geometrically. It is\nnot the measure of the union of all gaps: level <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> translated gaps of length <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>. The corresponding weighted sum and\nits geometric interpretation are given after Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257hg-i2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257hg-i2\">6.118</a>. Neither estimate\ndecides membership of a specified point such as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:half-divisor-unit-drop","The effect of adding one divisor","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and let finite supports\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></span> differ only by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>. For every positive integer\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In particular, the difference is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This follows directly from the\ndefinition of a divisor count. Its use in another coefficient sequence\nrequires proving that sequence has the same support-incidence\ndescription.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:tempered-orbit-rigidity","Uniqueness of an integer recurrence with\na vanishing scaled limit","<p><em> For any nonnegative-integer coefficient\nsequence <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>\nwith <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span>: the binary coefficient series <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></munderover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span> is\nrational iff there exists a positive integer multiplier <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> and an integer orbit <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> satisfying the\nexact carry recurrence <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> together with the condition <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Every integer sequence\nsatisfying both conditions obeys <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> exactly, where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup></math></span> is the\nscaled tail. For each fixed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> there\nis at most one such sequence. Positivity of the orbit alone is\ndeliberately <em>not</em> used as an equivalent criterion: a homogeneous\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></math></span>-scaled perturbation can be\nadded to any orbit without breaking the recurrence, so the limit\ncondition cannot be dropped. The choices <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></math></span> for Problem 257 and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi></math></span> for Problem 249 both satisfy\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></math></span>. The telescoping\nargument is given again, with an example, in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-t7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7\">6.105</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:tail-transfer","Changing finitely many support elements","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> be an integer, and\nlet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>.\nIf <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is irrational and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span> have finite symmetric difference,\nthen <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is irrational too: the\ntwo sums differ by a finite sum of rational numbers. This observation\nsupplies the finite modifications in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:eventually-periodic\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:eventually-periodic\">6.21</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:dyadic-excess-reformulation","An integer test for the interval between\nthe dyadic and Mersenne weights","<p><em> For positive integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, write the residual as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span>. Clearing positive denominators\ngives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The first\ntest concerns the dyadic bound, not the greedy selection threshold\nitself. The second identifies precisely the interval in which a Mersenne\nweight is skipped but the dyadic safety test fails.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:denominator-sandwich","The odd denominator survives dyadic\nsubtraction","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span> be a\nreduced fraction with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> odd,\nlet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> be an integer. The reduced\ndenominator of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span> is\ndivisible by <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span> and divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mi>D</mi></math></span>. Indeed, its unreduced numerator\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mi>r</mi></math></span> is coprime to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span>, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Reduction can\ntherefore remove only powers of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span>\nfrom the displayed denominator. No property of Mersenne weights is\nused.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:denominator-survival","A guaranteed divisor of a reduced\ndenominator","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Write <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for the positive\nreduced denominator of a rational number <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span>. If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, then\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. If\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>∣</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThus scaling may remove part of the guaranteed divisor. Indeed,\nreduction divides <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span> by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, so no prime power in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span> can be lost. After scaling, a prime\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>C</mi></math></span> can lose at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> powers from the guaranteed\ndivisor. This is a lower bound on the reduced denominator, not an\nequality: for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, it guarantees the divisor <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span>, while the reduced denominator is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>6</mn></math></span>. The lemma feeds Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:mersenne-channel-survival\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mersenne-channel-survival\">6.24</a>\ndirectly.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:mixed-prime-power-layer","Commuting prime-power differences","<p><em>\nFor a function <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, a positive integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span>\nand an integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, define </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThese operators commute. If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span>\nare primes, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus the mixed\ndifference counts precisely the support elements whose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span>- and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span>-adic exponents are <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>f</mi></math></span> in this divisor sum.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:achievement-set-topology","Achievement-set topology: compact,\nclosed, perfect, measure exactly one","<p><em> <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span> is compact\n(continuous image of the binary-sequence Cantor space <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>Fin</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn></math></span> under the\nproduct topology, via the positive-index digit map), hence closed; it is\nalso perfect, totally disconnected, and nowhere dense, with Lebesgue\nmeasure exactly <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The\ncompactness/closedness argument (binary coding <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></math></span> Cantor space <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></math></span> continuous image) is a fully generic\ntechnique for characterizing the achievement set of <em>any</em>\nabsolutely convergent digit-weighted series, not specific to Mersenne\ndenominators ; reusable for a <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span> subsum set after checking\nsummability. No separation of successive weights is needed for\ncompactness or closedness; the stronger topological conclusions require\ntheir own hypotheses. Closedness alone is what powers every “limit of a\nsequence of achieved points is achieved” argument in this catalogue\n(e.g. Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:seam-limit\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:seam-limit\">6.2</a>,\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:straddle-closed-set\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:straddle-closed-set\">6.5</a>).</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=09745a86500d"}
